.Dd September 23, 2026
.Dt ICOWM 1
.Os icowm-1.0.1
.Sh NAME
.Nm icowm
.Nd minimalist stacking window manager with desktop icons
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl hv
.Op Fl C
.Op Fl c Ar config_dir
.Op Fl d Ar display
.Op Fl L Ar log_level
.Op Fl l Ar log_file
.Op Fl M Ar mib
.Op Fl qrst
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is a minimalist stacking window manager for the X Window System.
It manages ordinary, freely overlapping windows: there is no tiling and
no automatic layout of application windows.
An iconified window is represented as an actual icon placed on the
desktop, in the manner of classic window managers such as
.Xr twm 1 ,
rather than as an entry in a taskbar.
.Pp
Configuration lives in a set of JSON files under a single directory.
None of them is required: a value absent from a file, or a file absent
altogether, falls back to a built-in default, so
.Nm
starts and runs with no configuration at all.
See
.Sx FILES
below, and the section 5 pages listed under
.Sx SEE ALSO
for each file's own format.
.Pp
.Nm
also exposes a local control socket, so a script can query its state or
drive it without going through X11 client messages.
See
.Xr icowm-msg 1 .
.Ss Options
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl C
Check every configuration file under the resolved configuration
directory for JSON syntax errors, report the result, and exit without
starting a session.
.It Fl c Ar config_dir
Read configuration from
.Ar config_dir ,
overriding the resolution order described under
.Sx FILES .
.It Fl d Ar display
Connect to
.Ar display ,
for instance
.Ql :0 .
When absent, the
.Ev DISPLAY
environment variable is used.
.It Fl h
Print usage information and exit.
.It Fl L Ar log_level
Set the minimum log verbosity, from 0 to 8, running from
.Ql trace
through
.Ql fatal .
.Nm Fl h
prints the level names in full.
.It Fl l Ar log_file
Set the log destination: a file path, or one of the keywords
.Ql DEFAULT ,
which sends errors to standard error and informational messages to
standard output,
.Ql STDOUT ,
.Ql STDERR ,
or
.Ql NULL
to disable logging altogether.
.It Fl M Ar mib
Run in restricted-memory mode with a ceiling of
.Ar mib
mebibytes.
The value must be at least 14.
See
.Sx Restricted-memory mode
below.
.It Fl q
Log fatal errors only, which is identical to the highest
.Fl L
level.
.It Fl r
Replace an already-running window manager.
Without this,
.Nm
refuses to start when another window manager already owns the
.Ql WM_S Ns Ar n
selection on the display; with it,
.Nm
claims that selection and waits for the previous owner to relinquish it.
.It Fl s
Disable the control socket for this run.
The socket is never set up at all, rather than being attempted and
possibly failing.
.It Fl t
Record the originating function in every logged message, not only at the
.Ql trace
level.
.It Fl v
Print version and license information, and exit.
.El
.Ss Exiting
There are two ways to shut
.Nm
down, and they behave differently on purpose.
.Pp
The ordinary quit action, whether reached through its key binding, its
confirmation dialog, or the root menu, asks every open application to
close itself first, the same request that closing one window
individually already sends, so an application with unsaved work gets the
same chance to warn about it that it gets at any other time.
.Nm
waits for all of them, up to the configured shutdown timeout, before
exiting.
.Pp
The emergency exit shortcut,
.Ql Ctrl+Mod1+Backspace ,
disabled by default, does none of that.
It runs with no dialog, no confirmation and no wait: the moment it is
pressed,
.Nm
terminates, skipping even the exit session hooks that the ordinary quit
action runs.
This is deliberate.
The shortcut exists for situations where
.Nm
itself might be unresponsive or in some broken state, so it is kept to
the smallest, most direct action possible: any step that waited on the
event loop or on rendering would only be as reliable as whatever it is
that might be the very reason someone reaches for this shortcut.
.Ss Restricted-memory mode
.Nm Fl M Ar mib
aims at genuinely memory-constrained systems: an old machine, a
low-power single-board computer, a virtual machine given very little
RAM.
.Pp
Before doing anything else,
.Nm
checks how much memory the system has free.
If that is less than
.Ar mib ,
it does not start at all, and says so in the log: promising to stay
under a ceiling is not meaningful if the system cannot spare that much
to begin with.
.Pp
Once running,
.Nm
watches its own memory use.
On reaching the ceiling, a dialog reports how much is in use and
suggests closing a window before opening anything else.
This is a warning rather than a wall: nothing is forcibly cut off at
that number.
.Pp
The mode also caps how many windows are managed at once, locks the
session to a single desktop, and reads its own configuration file in
place of the ordinary one.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
.It Ev DISPLAY
The X display to connect to, when
.Fl d
is not given.
.It Ev XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Base directory for configuration; see
.Sx FILES .
.It Ev HOME
Used to resolve the configuration directory when
.Ev XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is unset.
.It Ev LC_ALL , Ev LC_MESSAGES , Ev LC_CTYPE , Ev LC_TIME , Ev LANG
.Nm
sets every locale category from the environment at startup, so these
are consulted in the usual order: for each category,
.Ev LC_ALL
if set, then that category's own variable, then
.Ev LANG .
.Pp
.Ev LC_MESSAGES
picks which translation of the interface is used, and
.Ev LC_CTYPE
decides the encoding the translated text comes back in, so a locale
naming no encoding, or none at all, leaves accented characters
replaced even where a translation exists.
.Ev LC_TIME
is what the system tray clock's own format is rendered against.
.It Ev LANGUAGE
Not consulted by
.Nm
itself, but honored by GNU gettext, where it takes precedence over
every variable above for the choice of translation alone.
It holds a colon-separated list of catalogue names matched literally,
so
.Ql es
finds a catalogue while
.Ql es_ES.UTF-8
does not, unlike
.Ev LANG ,
which falls back through the shorter forms.
A session that already sets this will not change its interface
language through
.Ev LANG
or
.Ev LC_ALL .
.El
.Sh FILES
The configuration directory is resolved in this order: the argument to
.Fl c ,
if given; otherwise
.Pa ${XDG_CONFIG_HOME}/icowm ;
otherwise
.Pa ${HOME}/.icowm ;
otherwise
.Pa ./.icowm
in the current working directory.
Within it:
.Bl -tag -width themes/
.It Pa config.json
Base configuration: screens, desktops, windows, focus and placement
policy, icons, menus, the system tray and the scratchpad.
.It Pa bindings.json
Keyboard and mouse bindings.
.It Pa themes/
One JSON file per theme, of which one is selected from
.Pa config.json .
.It Pa randr.json
XRandR output profiles: resolution, position, rotation, and which
output is primary.
.It Pa a11y.json
Timing and visual-feedback overrides.
.It Pa rules.json
Per-window rules, matched when a window is mapped.
.It Pa session.json
Commands run at session start and at session end.
.It Pa menu.json
Contents of the root desktop menu.
.It Pa memguard.json
Configuration used in place of
.Pa config.json
under
.Fl M .
.El
.Pp
Translations live apart from the configuration, one directory per
language, named the short way so that both
.Ev LANG
and
.Ev LANGUAGE
find them:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa locale/<lang>/LC_MESSAGES/icowm.mo
The compiled catalogue for language
.Pa lang .
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It 0
Normal exit, through the quit action, the emergency shortcut,
.Dv SIGTERM ,
or the control socket.
.Fl h
and
.Fl v
also exit with 0.
.It -1
An unrecognized option, or an argument that could not be used.
.It 1
Out of memory before the session could start.
.It 2
The X display could not be opened, or the text renderer could not be set
up on it.
.It 3
The configuration could not be initialized.
.It 5
The X server reported no screens.
.It 6
Out of memory while allocating the surface list.
.It 7
A surface could not be initialized.
.It 8
A surface could not be recorded.
.It 9
Root window events could not be subscribed to, which normally means
another window manager is already running.
.It 10
The EWMH atoms could not be interned.
.It 11
Restricted-memory mode found less free memory than its own ceiling.
.It 12
The manager selection could not be acquired: another window manager owns
it and
.Fl r
was not given, or the previous owner did not release it in time.
.El
.Pp
Status 4 is not used.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Start from an
.Pa ~/.xinitrc ,
which is the usual way:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
exec icowm
.Ed
.Pp
Check the configuration without starting a session, which is what a
packaging script or an editor hook would run:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
icowm -C -c ~/.config/icowm
.Ed
.Pp
Run on a small machine with a ceiling of 24 mebibytes, logging to a
file:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
exec icowm -M 24 -l /tmp/icowm.log
.Ed
.Pp
Replace a window manager already running on the display:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
icowm -r
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr icowm-msg 1 ,
.Xr icowm 5 ,
.Xr icowm-a11y 5 ,
.Xr icowm-bindings 5 ,
.Xr icowm-memguard 5 ,
.Xr icowm-menu 5 ,
.Xr icowm-randr 5 ,
.Xr icowm-rules 5 ,
.Xr icowm-session 5 ,
.Xr icowm-theme 5
.Sh STANDARDS
.Nm
follows the
.Rs
.%T Extended Window Manager Hints
.Re
specification, publishing
.Ql _NET_SUPPORTED ,
.Ql _NET_ACTIVE_WINDOW ,
.Ql _NET_WM_DESKTOP ,
.Ql _NET_WM_STATE ,
.Ql _NET_WM_STRUT_PARTIAL ,
.Ql _NET_WM_PING
and their kindred atoms, so panels, taskbars and pagers work with it.
An incoming
.Ql _NET_ACTIVE_WINDOW
request is weighed against whichever client already holds focus before
being honored, so a window idle for some while cannot seize the
keyboard from whatever the user is presently engaged with.
.Pp
It likewise follows the
.Rs
.%T Inter-Client Communication Conventions Manual
.Re
in its treatment of
.Ql WM_DELETE_WINDOW ,
.Ql WM_TAKE_FOCUS ,
.Ql WM_TRANSIENT_FOR ,
.Ql WM_NORMAL_HINTS ,
.Ql WM_HINTS
and
.Ql WM_PROTOCOLS ,
so that both modern and legacy X11 applications behave correctly.
A window's
.Ql WM_TRANSIENT_FOR
descendants, its dialogs and the like, are raised along with it, so none
is left stranded beneath the window it belongs to.
.Pp
The source is strict C99 and POSIX.
.Sh HISTORY
.Nm
first appeared in version 1.0, together with
.Xr icowm-msg 1 .
.Pp
It was written out of a wish for a window manager of moderate appetite
that brought back the old-school notion of iconification, a window
becoming an icon on the desktop, in place of the minimization that
hides a window into a taskbar and leaves nothing of it in view.
